OffloadingConnector under dflash: stop eagle-flagging every KV group, and
warn on the chunk-geometry that silently zeroes cross-request reuse (#33).

Two findings from tracing the "KVarN stores 41 GB, loads exactly 0 bytes"
report (issue #33) on this card, with the connector instrumented:

1. Upstream only ever sets is_eagle_group for DeepSeek V4, and the
   connector's fallback marks EVERY group as draft attention whenever
   speculative_config.use_eagle() is true -- which includes method=dflash and
   mtp. Every group then has its trailing chunk excluded from store while
   decoding ("EAGLE/MTP draft attention groups [0..8] detected" at boot).
   For dflash the draft model's KV is exactly its sliding-window layers, so
   the fallback now flags SW-spec groups only; boot line reads "[8]". Under
   other eagle-family methods the upstream fallback is unchanged.

2. The actual zero-loads mechanism, reproduced and traced here (KVarN
   CTX=huge + TieringOffloadingSpec, 4 GiB CPU tier): the CPU pool allocates
   UNIFORM blocks sized for the largest group's chunk (2,176 tokens ->
   ~14.6 MiB blocks, 293 of them in 4.28 GiB). Under KVarN the promoted
   drafter SW group's chunks are 128 tokens -- each one still occupies a
   full block, so a single 23k-token request consumes ~264 blocks (~184 of
   them for tiny SW chunks) and EVICTS THE ENTIRE TIER. Store succeeds,
   complete_store succeeds, and by the time the same document returns, its
   blocks are gone: every lookup MISS, 0 bytes ever loaded, exactly the
   report. On bf16 the SW group shares the large block size (the same
   4096-B/token coincidence hybrid-sw-block-promote.patch documents), so
   the geometry never explodes and offloading uplifts -- which is why the
   reporter saw the split along KV dtype.

   The real fix (per-group block sizing, or excluding never-hittable SW
   groups from both store and lookup) is upstream-shaped; until then the
   config builder computes the waste factor and WARNS at boot with the
   arithmetic (group, chunk tokens, blocks-per-chunk ratio, and the
   cpu_bytes_to_use multiplier that would compensate), so the next person
   gets the diagnosis in their log instead of a day of silence.

Verified on this card: boot line shows eagle groups [8]; the warning fires
for group 8 (128 vs 2,176 tokens, 17x) on KVarN + connector and stays
silent on configs with uniform chunk sizes.

